{"abstract":"Engaging in the deliberate generation of abnormal outputs from Large Language Models (LLMs) by attacking them is a novel human activity. This paper presents a thorough exposition of how and why people perform such attacks, defining LLM red-teaming based on extensive and diverse evidence. Using a formal qualitative methodology, we interviewed dozens of practitioners from a broad range of backgrounds, all contributors to this novel work of attempting to cause LLMs to fail. We focused on the research questions of defining LLM red teaming, uncovering the motivations and goals for performing the activity, and characterizing the strategies people use when attacking LLMs. Based on the data, LLM red teaming is defined as a limit-seeking, non-malicious, manual activity, which depends highly on a team-effort and an alchemist mindset. It is highly intrinsically motivated by curiosity, fun, and to some degrees by concerns for various harms of deploying LLMs. We identify a taxonomy of 12 strategies and 35 different techniques of attacking LLMs. These findings are presented as a comprehensive grounded theory of how and why people attack large language models: LLM red teaming.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Derczynski, Leon","Stray, Jonathan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798542"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","NVIDIA","University of California, Berkeley","University of Washington"],"keyword":["Large Language Models","Qualitative methodology"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"summon a demon and bind it a grounded theory of llm red teaming","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314658","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798542","identifier":[{"id":"85215128727","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-820a9387-38ad-4056-93ca-7d24d10a4111"],"keyword":["Abnormal output generation","LLM red-teaming","Large Language Models","Limit-seeking strategies","Qualitative methodology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-1A033932B4C58A09AF7F27EB4476520D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314658","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Software Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of washington","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Washington","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"nvidia","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"NVIDIA","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"university of california berkeley","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California, Berkeley","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon","key":"derczynskipau","last":"Derczynski","locid":"785a7089-e58e-4aaa-986d-ae0e615ba508","name":"Derczynski, Leon","orcid":"0000-0002-8656-3431","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonathan","key":"straypau","last":"Stray","name":"Stray, Jonathan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0314658","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"1","journal":"PLOS ONE","paperid":"e0314658","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","volume":"20"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85215128727"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-820a9387-38ad-4056-93ca-7d24d10a4111"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Summon a demon and bind it: A grounded theory of LLM red teaming","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-798542","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"The energy cost of developing and deploying Generative AI (GenAI) models has exploded with their mass adoption, as has the ensuing carbon emissions. The climate impact of this is currently unknown. In Human-Computer Interaction, GenAI models are rarely trained but often used. Based on detailed review of 282 papers, we estimate this footprint from energy consumption of the total use of GenAI for CHI 2024 research as between 10,769.63 and 10,925.12 kg CO2e - equal to driving a car for more than 100,000 km. We show that in CHI research, GenAI is most often used for Prototyping, Evaluation & User studies, and that Data Collection and Fine-tuning models incurs the highest CO2st.1 We find that CHI submissions are unlikely to report GenAI use transparently, which makes precise calculations difficult. By measuring the usage of a subset of the papers on local hardware, we obtain estimations of the energy consumption and carbon footprint. Based on this evidence, we discuss and demonstrate ways to mitigate the issues of GenAI carbon footprint and lack of transparency.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Falk, Jeanette","Selvan, Raghavendra"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-814656"],"event":{"end":"2025-05-01","place":"Yokohama, jp","start":"2025-04-26","title":"2025 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems, CHI 2025","year":"2025"},"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["AI Hype","Carbon footprint","Energy consumption","Environmental sustainability","Generative AI"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how co000000000002stly is chi the carbon footprint of generative ai in hci research and what we should do about it","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3706598.3714227","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-814656","identifier":[{"id":"105005758403","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-90220790-5401-4756-b521-014d76f0a025"],"keyword":["AI Hype","Environmental Sustainability","Generative AI","carbon footprint","energy consumption"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-66D695CC7EE1C5AD1A12CA7AACD58C09"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/794474974/kl.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3706598.3714227","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centered Computing","Aalborg University, [Mission] Improved Wellbeing Among Children And Youth In Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","name":"Inie, Nanna","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jeanette","key":"falkpau","last":"Falk","locid":"5084b27d-c3fe-43a4-9fe3-3ecd28908952","name":"Falk, Jeanette","orcid":"0000-0001-7278-9344","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Raghavendra","key":"selvanpau","last":"Selvan","name":"Selvan, Raghavendra","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3706598.3714227","eisbn":["9798400713941"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","series":"Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems - Proceedings","status":"p","title":"CHI 2025 - Proceedings of the 2025 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105005758403"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-90220790-5401-4756-b521-014d76f0a025"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"How CO2STLY Is CHI? the Carbon Footprint of Generative AI in HCI Research and What We Should Do about It","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-814656","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"In this paper we investigate how people’s level of trust (as reported through self-assessment) in so-called “AI” (artificial intelligence) is influenced by anthropomorphizing language in system descriptions. Building on prior work, we define four categories of anthropomorphization (1. Properties of a cognizer, 2. Agency, 3. Biological metaphors, and 4. Properties of a communicator). We use a survey-based approach (=954) to investigate whether participants are likely to trust one of two (fictitious) “AI” systems by randomly assigning people to see either an anthropomorphized or a de-anthropomorphized description of the systems. We find that participants are no more likely to trust anthropomorphized over de-anthropmorphized product descriptions overall. The type of product or system in combination with different anthropomorphic categories appears to exert greater influence on trust than anthropomorphizing language alone, and age is the only demographic factor that significantly correlates with people’s preference for anthropomorphized or de-anthropomorphized descriptions. When elaborating on their choices, participants highlight factors such as lesser of two evils, lower or higher stakes contexts, and human favoritism as driving motivations when choosing between product A and B, irrespective of whether they saw an anthropomorphized or a de-anthropomorphized description of the product. Our results suggest that “anthropomorphism” in “AI” descriptions is an aggregate concept that may influence different groups differently, and provide nuance to the discussion of whether anthropomorphization leads to higher trust and over-reliance by the general public in systems sold as “AI”.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Druga, Stefania","Zukerman, Peter","Bender, Emily M."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776432"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","The University of Chicago","University of Washington"],"keyword":["AI","Anthropomorphism","Semantics","Trust"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"from ai to probabilistic automation how does anthropomorphization of technical systems descriptions influence trust","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3630106.3659040","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776432","identifier":[{"id":"85195061019","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-a23063f1-2983-40aa-ad79-64c53cc08225"],"keyword":["AI","anthropomorphism","probabilistic automation","semantics","trust"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-48E23432BF482FF96906189B09D15D17"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3630106.3659040","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"the university of chicago","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"The University of Chicago","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of washington","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Washington","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefania","key":"drugapau","last":"Druga","name":"Druga, Stefania","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter","key":"zukermanpau","last":"Zukerman","name":"Zukerman, Peter","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Emily M.","key":"benderpau","last":"Bender","name":"Bender, Emily M.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3630106.3659040","pages":"2322-2347","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85195061019"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a23063f1-2983-40aa-ad79-64c53cc08225"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"From \"AI\" to Probabilistic Automation: How Does Anthropomorphization of Technical Systems Descriptions Influence Trust?","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776432","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The underrepresentation of women in IT has been a long-standing issue. Research has shown that women’s first experience with computing education is vital in shaping their perception of the field and their perception of who “belongs” to it. In this Danish case study, we examine gender representation in the subject Informatics, which is the first mandatory, formal introduction to computing education for many students in Denmark. Across 25% of all Danish HHX high schools, we assess the teaching materials on parameters of gender representation in pronouns, names, images, audio/video material, and potential role models. Our results suggest an overall underrepresentation of women in textual and audio/video material, but not in images. We also find an overall absence of female role models, and a tendency for the materials to feature younger female role models than their male counterparts. We conclude with concrete recommendations for authors and publishers of teaching materials for ICT on the high school level.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Christensen, Ingrid Maria","Marcher, Melissa Høegh","Inie, Nanna","Brabrand, Claus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-759398"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Computing education"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"invisible women in it examining gender representation in k 000000000012 ict teaching materials","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3632620.3671111","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-759398","identifier":[{"id":"85202896996","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-426120e3-a659-422b-8a15-5d2df9a8fb9c"],"keyword":["Computing education"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D91C5B59EF21BE309DC78BD37F6482D2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3632620.3671111","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3632620.3671111","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ingrid Maria","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Ingrid Maria","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Melissa Høegh","key":"marcherpau","last":"Marcher","locid":"2f4daeb6-5872-4c86-8f2d-5c5ffc3dd5fa","name":"Marcher, Melissa Høegh","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Claus","key":"brabrandpau","last":"Brabrand","locid":"47a94200-3abe-4354-b5e3-c8ef6f1ba1f2","name":"Brabrand, Claus","orcid":"0000-0001-5093-5806","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Paul","key":"dennypedh","last":"Denny","name":"Denny, Paul","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Leo","key":"porterpedh","last":"Porter","name":"Porter, Leo","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Margaret","key":"hamiltonpedh","last":"Hamilton","name":"Hamilton, Margaret","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Briana","key":"morrisonpedh","last":"Morrison","name":"Morrison, Briana","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3632620.3671111","eisbn":["9798400704758"],"pages":"246-262","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2024 ACM Conference on International Computing Education Research (ICER 2024)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85202896996"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-426120e3-a659-422b-8a15-5d2df9a8fb9c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Invisible Women in IT: Examining Gender Representation in K-12 ICT Teaching Materials","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-759398","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The recent leaps in complexity and fluency of Large Language Models (LLMs) mean that, for the first time in human history, people can interact with computers using natural language alone. This creates monumental possibilities of automation and accessibility of computing, but also raises severe security and safety threats: When everyone can interact with LLMs, everyone can potentially break into the systems running LLMs. All it takes is creative use of language. This paper presents Hacc-Man, a game which challenges its players to \"jailbreak\"an LLM: subvert the LLM to output something that it is not intended to. Jailbreaking is at the intersection between creative problem solving and LLM security. The purpose of the game is threefold: 1. To heighten awareness of the risks of deploying fragile LLMs in everyday systems, 2. To heighten people's self-efficacy in interacting with LLMs, and 3. To discover the creative problem solving strategies, people deploy in this novel context.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Valentim, Matheus","Falk, Jeanette","Inie, Nanna"],"cuid":["S02-083.60-767050"],"event":{"end":"2024-06-05","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2024-06-01","title":"ACM Conference on Designing Interactive Systems 2024","year":"2024"},"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Creative problem solving","Creativity","Hacking","LLM Security","arcade games","jailbreaking","red teaming"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hacc man an arcade game for jailbreaking llms","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3656156.3665432","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-083.60-767050","identifier":[{"id":"85198900811","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-d4e09114-48ca-48e7-a99e-5487589adead","itu-edfb6f9b-47d4-4cb7-90ca-81c2d2e5350d"],"keyword":["LLM security","arcade games.","creative problem solving","creativity","creativity, creative problem solving","hacking","jailbreaking","red teaming"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-1C50D525E2B9187CABF608774AE6814C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/753240355/3656156.3665432.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3656156.3665432","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centered Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Matheus","key":"valentimpau","last":"Valentim","name":"Valentim, Matheus","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jeanette","key":"falkpau","last":"Falk","locid":"5084b27d-c3fe-43a4-9fe3-3ecd28908952","name":"Falk, Jeanette","orcid":"0000-0001-7278-9344","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Anna","key":"vallgårdapedh","last":"Vallgårda","name":"Vallgårda, Anna","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Li","key":"jönssonpedh","last":"Jönsson","name":"Jönsson, Li","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jonas","key":"fritschpedh","last":"Fritsch","name":"Fritsch, Jonas","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3656156.3665432","eisbn":["9798400706325"],"pages":"338-341","place":"New York, USA","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery (ACM)","status":"p","title":"DIS 2024 - Proceedings of the 2024 ACM Designing Interactive Systems Conference"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85198900811"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-d4e09114-48ca-48e7-a99e-5487589adead","itu-edfb6f9b-47d4-4cb7-90ca-81c2d2e5350d"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Hacc-Man : An Arcade Game for Jailbreaking LLMs","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-083.60-767050","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Abundant research has investigated gender differ- ences from the perspective of the individual, but little research has explored gender differences from a group perspective. This paper presents a survey-based quantitative exploration of gender differences when N=138 students working in smaller (3 stu- dents) groups that are either men-majority (WMM) or men- only (MMM). The study was conducted during four weeks of CS1 project work, and the investigation focused on student struggle, task division, group satisfaction, and course outcomes. We observed four significant gender differences: (i) Many women students express a lack of confidence early in the project work, as do many men students that are part of mixed-gender (WMM) groups, but not men that are part of men-only (MMM) groups; (ii) In men-majority groups, women perform more ‘non- technical’ tasks than men (e.g., analysis, design, and report editing); and (iii) women students in men-majority groups are less satisfied with several aspects of the group work than men students in the same groups. These findings add to our understanding of group dynamics and collaboration (including what to avoid and where to pay extra attention) when assigning projects in smaller groups in computing education.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Westh, Bjørn Hjorth","Inie, Nanna","Barkhuus, Louise","Brabrand, Claus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718933"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Computing education","Confidence in project work","Gender differences","Group dynamics","Group satisfaction","Men majority groups","Mixed gender groups","Non technical tasks","Project work outcomes","Task division"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"gender differences in the group dynamics of smaller cs000000000001 project groups","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102753129/FIE-2023-struggle_pdf.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This project is partially funded by DIREC P31 with the intention of uncovering knowledge important for the recruitment and retention of students in Computer Science. One recurring discussion is how to form student groups and whether to let students form groups autonomously or to have course instructors form groups (and on which basis). This study was part of an initiative to understand how these groups work in practice, in order to be able to make evidence-based recommendations and decisions about this in the future."],"id":"S01-000.00-718933","ids":["itu-f48d14a6-4f0b-4ef6-85f9-64b989c0ebb1"],"keyword":["Computing education","Confidence in project work","Gender differences","Group dynamics","Group satisfaction","Men-majority groups","Mixed-gender groups","Non-technical tasks","Project work outcomes","Task division"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-61C0194325C7E4E2D053065FF7A5E20D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102753129/FIE-2023-struggle_pdf.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://ccer.itu.dk/-/media/86E7BD9D7CD147E3AE014795C9AE42D3.ashx","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Bjørn Hjorth","key":"westhpau","last":"Westh","name":"Westh, Bjørn Hjorth","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise","key":"barkhuuspau","last":"Barkhuus","locid":"831421c0-027f-4a25-b795-ee2fce0c8d2a","name":"Barkhuus, Louise","orcid":"0000-0003-1306-249X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Claus","key":"brabrandpau","last":"Brabrand","locid":"47a94200-3abe-4354-b5e3-c8ef6f1ba1f2","name":"Brabrand, Claus","orcid":"0000-0001-5093-5806","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"place":"College Station, Texas, USA","status":"p","title":"IEEE ASEE Frontiers in Education Conference 2023"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f48d14a6-4f0b-4ef6-85f9-64b989c0ebb1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Gender Differences in the Group Dynamics of Smaller CS1 Project Groups","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718933","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"When humans are exposed to environmental and physical stressors, cognitive performance is degraded. Even though several studies have examined the effect of various stressors individually, there are limited studies comparing the impact of different types. This study examined the effects of Heat, Noise, and Alcohol on cognitive performance during two programming tasks to quantify the impact of stressors on novice programmers. The experiment enrolled N=100 university student volunteers for a between-subjects experiment. Participants were randomly assigned to one of four conditions (M=25): a room with at 38 °C (100 °F), a room with conversational noise around 80 dBA, a blood alcohol content of 1.0‰, or a base condition. Two programming tasks were administered: one analysis task (reading programs) and one synthesis task (writing programs), taking about half an hour to complete in total. Short-term exposure to heat appears to not significantly affect neither reading nor writing programs; conversational noise significantly impacts analytical tasks but not synthesis tasks; while alcohol significantly worsens performance in both analytical and synthesis tasks. To provide a tangible summary for decision-makers able to influence conditions for novice programmers, an approximated comparison is provided, which “translates” negative cognitive effects of heat, noise, and alcohol to one another.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Brabrand, Claus","Tell, Paolo","Inie, Nanna"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718852"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cognitive performance","Environmental stressors","Physical stressors","Programming","Software Engineering"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"programming under the influence on the effect of heat noise and alcohol on novice programmers","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102793509/Programming_under_the_influence_On_the_effect_of_Heat_Noise_and_Alcohol_on_novice_programmers.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718852","identifier":[{"id":"85182417093","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-492a7d2d-58f2-4663-8c3b-5d2f30baa6f7"],"keyword":["Cognitive performance","Environmental stressors","Physical stressors","Programming","Software engineering"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A5EEDA45CB17786CF6EE1AD86A422E1F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102793509/Programming_under_the_influence_On_the_effect_of_Heat_Noise_and_Alcohol_on_novice_programmers.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Claus","key":"brabrandpau","last":"Brabrand","locid":"47a94200-3abe-4354-b5e3-c8ef6f1ba1f2","name":"Brabrand, Claus","orcid":"0000-0001-5093-5806","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"tellpau","last":"Tell","locid":"2b8ff3a4-e9b0-403d-8bd8-1bf26121c234","name":"Tell, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0001-7391-0046","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jss.2023.111887","issn":["01641212","18731228"],"issn_org":["01641212"],"issue":"111887","journal":"Journal of Systems and Software","paperid":"111887","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Journal of Systems and Software","volume":"210"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85182417093"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-492a7d2d-58f2-4663-8c3b-5d2f30baa6f7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Programming under the influence: On the effect of Heat, Noise, and Alcohol on Novice programmers","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718852","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Generative AI, i.e., the group of technologies that automatically generate visual or written content based on text prompts, has un- dergone a leap in complexity and become widely available within just a few years. Such technologies potentially introduce a massive disruption to creative fields. This paper presents the results of a qualitative survey ( = 23) investigating how creative professionals think about generative AI. The results show that the advancement of these AI models prompts important reflections on what defines creativity and how creatives imagine using AI to support their workflows. Based on these reflections, we discuss how we might design participatory AI in the domain of creative expertise with the goal of empowering creative professionals in their present and future coexistence with AI.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Falk, Jeanette","Tanimoto, Steven"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712549"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Salzburg","University of Washington"],"keyword":["Creativity support","Generative AI","Participatory Design","creative professionals","participatory AI"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"designing participatory ai creative professionals worries and expectations about generative ai","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3544549.3585657","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["VILLUM Foundation, grant 37176 (ATTiKA: Adaptive Tools for Technical Knowledge Acquisition) and the Austrian Science Fund (FWF) [P34226-N]."],"id":"S01-000.00-712549","identifier":[{"id":"85158142616","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b9fa3d23-72b0-44ce-8fe9-017ed1ffe579"],"keyword":["creative professionals","creativity support","generative AI","participatory AI","participatory design"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6B4429573242D37FD8EA9CF886FA50A6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102402921/arXiv_version.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3544549.3585657","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"at","key":"university of salzburg","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Salzburg","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of washington","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Washington","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeanette","key":"falkpau","last":"Falk","name":"Falk, Jeanette","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Steven","key":"tanimotopau","last":"Tanimoto","name":"Tanimoto, Steven","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3544549.3585657","pages":"1-8","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Extended Abstracts of the 2023 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85158142616"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b9fa3d23-72b0-44ce-8fe9-017ed1ffe579"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Designing Participatory AI: Creative Professionals’ Worries and Expectations about Generative AI","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712549","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Creativity is often highlighted as one of the most relevant competencies or skills of the 21st century. Teaching about theoretical underpinnings of creativity has therefore become relevant in numerous fields, from computer science to business management. If we consider creativity as a literacy, like writing or programming, it is important that people from different backgrounds can learn about the basic creativity concepts and how these might be manifested in practice. This article presents the design rationale of The Creativity Game, a simple online game intended to teach the player about some of the very basic properties or concepts in creativity theory: exploration, value, novelty, constraints, and transformation. The Creativity Game is a prototype presented here to spark conversation about how we teach creativity theory in a tangible way.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Tanimoto, Steven","Inie, Nanna"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718827"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Washington"],"keyword":["Creativity Competencies","Creativity Concepts","Creativity Game","Creativity education","Online Creativity Training"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the creativity game a game for teaching first steps of theoretical creativity","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85164013756","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718827","identifier":[{"id":"85164013756","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-140faae4-11c9-431b-a8ca-87d46771d135"],"keyword":["Creativity Competencies","Creativity Concepts","Creativity Education","Creativity Game","Online Creativity Training"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C0446A965735A1A0547C8EEA3D062142"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of washington","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Washington","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Steven","key":"tanimotopau","last":"Tanimoto","name":"Tanimoto, Steven","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3591196.3596826","isbn":["9798400701801"],"pages":"274-281","place":"New York","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"C&amp;C '23: Proceedings of the 15th Conference on Creativity and Cognition"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85164013756"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-140faae4-11c9-431b-a8ca-87d46771d135"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Creativity Game: A Game for Teaching First Steps of Theoretical Creativity","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718827","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"This paper explores redirection of activity as an intervention strategy for self-regulation online. We conducted an explorative study (N = 19) of the browser extension Aiki, which redirects a user from a self-defined ‘time-wasting’ website to an online platform for learning programming (Sololearn, Codecademy, or Udemy). Based on quantitative measures alone, using Aiki decreased the participants’ time spent on time-wasting websites on average, and increased programming knowledge. However, several users ended up avoiding their time-wasting websites entirely when Aiki was active, or they discontinued the use of the extension after ‘the novelty wore off’. Based on these effects, we qualitatively explored the user experiences and identified four challenges and four opportunities for using redirection of activity as an intervention strategy for self-regulation of time management in a browser. Our results suggest that this intervention strategy is promising, but careful design is necessary to strike an optimal balance between independence and regulation.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Westh, Bjørn Hjorth","Muller, John Henrik","Lungu, Mircea"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718890"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Browser extension","Redirection of activity","Self regulation online","Time management","User experience analysis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"challenges and opportunities of using redirection of activity for self regulation online","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102539595/chi23-651-access.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718890","identifier":[{"id":"85160015709","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-8dbec46b-156c-4d77-be71-b54b7ab6df38"],"keyword":["Browser extension","Redirection of activity","Self-regulation online","Time management","User experience analysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BD4EE2397687C55F0150E2C6F24DF203"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102539595/chi23-651-access.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bjørn Hjorth","key":"westhpau","last":"Westh","name":"Westh, Bjørn Hjorth","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"John Henrik","key":"mullerpau","last":"Muller","name":"Muller, John Henrik","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mircea","key":"lungupau","last":"Lungu","locid":"02960d47-7677-4b2c-99d4-490a31fd6885","name":"Lungu, Mircea","orcid":"0000-0003-3944-5261","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3544548.3581342","pages":"1-19","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI '23: Proceedings of the 2023 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85160015709"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8dbec46b-156c-4d77-be71-b54b7ab6df38"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Challenges and Opportunities of Using Redirection of Activity for Self-Regulation Online","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718890","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"In this article, we argue that game jam formats are uniquely suited to engage participants in learning about artificial intelligence (AI) as a design material because of four factors which are characteristic of game jams: 1) Game jams provide an opportunity for hands-on, interactive prototyping, 2) Game jams encourage playful participation, 3) Game jams encourage creative combinations of AI and game development, and 4) Game jams offer understandable goals and evaluation metrics for AI. We support the argument with an interview study conducted with three AI experts who had all organized game jams with a focus on using AI in game development. Based on a thematic analysis of the expert interviews and a theoretical background of Schön's work on educating the reflective practitioner, we identified the four abovementioned factors as well as four recommendations for structuring and planning an AI-focused game jam: 1) Aligning repertoires, 2) Supporting playful participation, 3) Supporting ideation, and 4) Facilitating evaluation and reflection. Our contribution is motivated by the recent discourse on general challenges and recommendations of teaching AI identified by related literature, here under the long and intertwined history of games and AI in general. The article presents an initial discussion of the value of game jam formats for learning about AI and which factors need to be considered in regard to this specific learning goal.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Falk, Jeanette","Inie, Nanna"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383960"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Salzburg"],"keyword":["AI","Knowledge acquisition","Learning","ML","game jams"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"materializing the abstract understanding ai by game jamming","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fcomp.2022.959351","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383960","identifier":[{"id":"85140088639","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b8402dbc-1e56-4666-9a2f-080d4af3cf6b"],"keyword":["AI","ML","game jams","knowledge acquisition","learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2F37B948636ED036F74A7D10AC211BAD"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fcomp.2022.959351","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"at","key":"university of salzburg","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Salzburg","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeanette","key":"falkpau","last":"Falk","name":"Falk, Jeanette","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fcomp.2022.959351","issn":["26249898"],"journal":"Frontiers in Computer Science","pages":"1-20","publisher":"Frontiers","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Computer Science"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85140088639"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b8402dbc-1e56-4666-9a2f-080d4af3cf6b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Materializing the Abstract: Understanding AI by Game Jamming","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383960","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"This paper presents and evaluates a new survey metric, the active Reading Support indeX (aRSX), which was created to help researchers and designers evaluate whether a specific software or hardware tool supports active reading of academic texts. The aRSX is comprised of questions in five categories: The Text, Cognitive Workload, Physical Workload, Perceived Learning, User Experience and Aesthetics, and Flow, as well as an open-ended question for additional comments. In this paper, we present our initial development of two beta-versions of the questionnaire in two studies of n = 100 and n = 53 deployments, evaluating paper, laptops, iPads, and reMarkable tablets as reading support tools. These studies led to the current version of the aRSX, and the initial results suggest that the metric is a reliable and valid indicator of a tool’s ability to support reading of academic texts.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Westh, Bjørn Hjorth"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383995"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cognitive workload","Reading support tools","User experience","active reading","survey metric"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evaluating academic reading support tools developing the arsx questionnaire","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92198309/Preprint_Evaluation_of_Academic_Reading_Support_Tools_Developing_the_aRSX_questionnaire.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-383995","identifier":[{"id":"85136921321","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-bab400b1-fa4a-4ced-91ad-b48af92d8f82"],"keyword":["active reading","cognitive workload","reading support tools","survey metric","user experience"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B0C1B42781B0E843455A436A700A8D8A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92198309/Preprint_Evaluation_of_Academic_Reading_Support_Tools_Developing_the_aRSX_questionnaire.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bjørn Hjorth","key":"westhpau","last":"Westh","name":"Westh, Bjørn Hjorth","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-14756-2_16","eisbn":["9783031147562"],"isbn":["9783031147555"],"pages":"316-335","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Communications in Computer and Information Science (CCIS, volume 1624)","volume":"1624"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85136921321"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-bab400b1-fa4a-4ced-91ad-b48af92d8f82"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Evaluating Academic Reading Support Tools: Developing the aRSX-Questionnaire","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383995","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"We present a long-term study of how university students experienced teaching/learning activities throughout the Covid-19 pandemic in Denmark1. We collected data through questionnaires from N=365 students enrolled in the “Intro- duction to Database Systems” course during four consecutive semesters (Spring 2020 to Fall 2021). The two years span the entire period of the pandemic’s interruption of normal on-site teaching, until restrictions were completely lifted in Denmark. The study investigates student preferences for online versus on- site teaching, and identifies the advantages of both, as well as changes in preferences throughout the pandemic. Quantitatively, the results demonstrate a preference for on-site over online teaching which was more pronounced for exercise classes than for lectures. Qualitatively, the study identifies several advantages of both online and on-site teaching; including a more engaging learning environment and better teacher-student interaction for on-site lectures, and flexibility and self-paced learning for online teaching. The primary changes identified were an increased sense of being able to focus online and a decrease in ease of asking questions online towards the later stages of the pandemic. Finally, we highlight the opportunity for universities to provide hybrid models of teaching, in order to care for diverse student preferences and needs.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Jónsson, Björn Thór","Pischetola, Magda","Inie, Nanna","Daniels, Mats","Brabrand, Claus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-430709"],"event":{"end":"2022-10-11","place":"Uppsala, se","start":"2022-10-08","title":"Frontiers in Education Conference 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2022-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Iceland","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Iceland","Uppsala University"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"student perspectives on on site versus online teaching throughout the covid 000000000019 pandemic","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/324956887/pandemic_pdf.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-430709","ids":["ku-3e610c3a-b6a4-4efe-a5be-4db9e2d18712"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-CDC8301A8BB5EA064DC0633177DD51E9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/324956887/pandemic_pdf.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://ccer.itu.dk/-/media/E166C300E514461E85C0EB6C89184197.ashx","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"is","key":"university of iceland","loc":["Iceland","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Iceland","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"se","key":"uppsala university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Uppsala University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Björn Thór","key":"jónssonpau","last":"Jónsson","name":"Jónsson, Björn Thór","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Magda","key":"pischetolapau","last":"Pischetola","locid":"227e791f-6e3e-410c-afd5-0c6dcb56aab9","name":"Pischetola, Magda","orcid":"0000-0001-6697-2118","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","name":"Inie, Nanna","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Mats","key":"danielspau","last":"Daniels","name":"Daniels, Mats","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Claus","key":"brabrandpau","last":"Brabrand","name":"Brabrand, Claus","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-3e610c3a-b6a4-4efe-a5be-4db9e2d18712"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Student Perspectives on On-site versus Online Teaching throughout the Covid-19 Pandemic","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-430709","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"We present a long-term study of how university students experienced teaching/learning activities throughout the Covid-19 pandemic in Denmark 1 . We collected data through questionnaires from N=365 students enrolled in the \"Introduction to Database Systems\" course during four consecutive semesters (Spring 2020 to Fall 2021). The two years span the entire period of the pandemic’s interruption of normal on-site teaching, until restrictions were completely lifted in Denmark. The study investigates student preferences for online versus onsite teaching, and identifies the advantages of both, as well as changes in preferences throughout the pandemic. Quantitatively, the results demonstrate a preference for on-site over online teaching which was more pronounced for exercise classes than for lectures. Qualitatively, the study identifies several advantages of both online and on-site teaching; including a more engaging learning environment and better teacher-student interaction for on-site lectures, and flexibility and self-paced learning for online teaching. The primary changes identified were an increased sense of being able to focus online and a decrease in ease of asking questions online towards the later stages of the pandemic. Finally, we highlight the opportunity for universities to provide hybrid models of teaching, in order to care for diverse student preferences and needs.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Jónsson, Björn Thór","Pischetola, Magda","Inie, Nanna","Daniels, Mats","Brabrand, Claus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776383"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Uppsala University"],"keyword":["Hybrid teaching"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"student perspectives on on site versus online teaching throughout the covid 000000000019 pandemic","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/106948081/Student_Perspectives_on_On_site_versus_O.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776383","identifier":[{"id":"85143784366","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-2db9de83-cdb3-49bf-b3a8-f4468a1e1800"],"keyword":["Covid19 pandemic","hybrid teaching","online teaching and learning","student preferences"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-960308C54A19D1837E9E82EBC88A6B29"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102946609/pandemic_pdf.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/106948081/Student_Perspectives_on_On_site_versus_O.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://ccer.itu.dk/-/media/E166C300E514461E85C0EB6C89184197.ashx","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"se","key":"uppsala university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Uppsala University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Björn Thór","key":"jónssonpau","last":"Jónsson","name":"Jónsson, Björn Thór","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Magda","key":"pischetolapau","last":"Pischetola","name":"Pischetola, Magda","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Mats","key":"danielspau","last":"Daniels","name":"Daniels, Mats","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Claus","key":"brabrandpau","last":"Brabrand","locid":"47a94200-3abe-4354-b5e3-c8ef6f1ba1f2","name":"Brabrand, Claus","orcid":"0000-0001-5093-5806","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/fie56618.2022.9962707","eisbn":["9781665462440"],"isbn":["9781665462457"],"pages":"1-9","place":"Uppsala, Sweden","series":"IEEE Frontiers in Education Conference","status":"p","title":"IEEE Frontiers in Education Conference (FIE 2022)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85143784366"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2db9de83-cdb3-49bf-b3a8-f4468a1e1800"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Student Perspectives on On-site versus Online Teaching throughout the Covid-19 Pandemic","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776383","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"ABSTRACT Research ideas are pivotal in research practice. While research domains, topics, and methods are often outlined by specific research fields, the process of capturing and developing research ideas is less categorical. Conceiving and developing research ideas requires continuous creative thinking, usually supported by various different tools, each more or less carefully selected by a researcher to fulfill a specific purpose. In this paper, we investigate the creative work practices of academic researchers, with a focus on the workflows and tools they employ to manage ideas. Through a qualitative survey (n=51) and in-depth interviews (n=18) with researchers from a wide range of fields, we identify and describe typical processes of managing research ideas, different types of research ideas (a research question or problem, a method, a hypothesis or antithesis, and a theory), properties of good research ideas, as well as potentials for tools and technology to support idea management for researchers.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Frich Pedersen, Jonas","Dalsgaard, Peter"],"cuid":["S02-087.85-020291"],"event":{"end":"2022-06-23","place":"Venice, it","start":"2022-06-20","title":"14th ACM Creativity and Cognition Conference, C and C 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2022-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Academic Research Tools","Cognition","Creative Work Practices","Creativity","Idea management","Qualitative Survey","Research Ideas","Science of science","Tools"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how researchers manage ideas","src":["au","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92198353/Preprint_How_Researchers_Manage_Ideas.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-087.85-020291","identifier":[{"id":"85133398625","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-68af7e50-8342-4958-aa8b-b85555650e45","itu-f0993937-fb00-4d91-83e2-ca3bfd0f6185"],"keyword":["Academic Research Tools","Creative Work Practices","Qualitative Survey","cognition","creativity","idea management","idea types","research ideas","science of science","tools"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-9C32FC51E5A75F2D9EB02CE306B4F5E8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92198353/Preprint_How_Researchers_Manage_Ideas.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Arts, School of Communication and Culture, School of Communication and Culture - Department of Digital Design and Information Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jonas","key":"frichpau","last":"Frich","locid":"1f394a37-c0c2-40b9-b2b5-45e5021746c1","name":"Frich Pedersen, Jonas","name_org":"Frich, Jonas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"07bc29a5-f5da-47d0-8290-fd9ab3659541","name":"Dalsgaard, Peter","orcid":"0000-0001-9884-7178","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3527927.3532813","eisbn":["9781450393270"],"pages":"83-96","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"C&amp;C '22: Creativity and Cognition"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85133398625"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-68af7e50-8342-4958-aa8b-b85555650e45","itu-f0993937-fb00-4d91-83e2-ca3bfd0f6185"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How Researchers Manage Ideas","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-087.85-020291","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Online misogyny, a category of online abusive language, has serious and harmful social consequences. Automatic detection of misogynistic language online, while imperative, poses complicated challenges to both data gathering, data annotation, and bias mitigation, as this type of data is linguistically complex and diverse. This paper makes three contributions in this area: Firstly, we describe the detailed design of our iterative annotation process and codebook. Secondly, we present a comprehensive taxonomy of labels for annotating misogyny in natural written language, and finally, we introduce a high-quality dataset of annotated posts sampled from social media posts.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Zeinert, Philine","Inie, Nanna","Derczynski, Leon"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381861"],"event":{"acronym":"IJCNPL","title":"International Joint Conference on Natural Language Processing"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Automatic detection","Bias Mitigation","Data Annotation","Online Misogyny","Social media analysis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"annotating online misogyny","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86115829/annotating_online_misogyny_1_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381861","ids":["itu-274d2228-a805-4763-994d-7c2951a0c8a1"],"keyword":["Automatic Detection","Bias Mitigation","Data Annotation","Online Misogyny","Social Media Analysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-6127DA6DD479E6BA729E0DE338CDF07A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86115829/annotating_online_misogyny_1_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Philine","key":"zeinertpau","last":"Zeinert","name":"Zeinert, Philine","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon","key":"derczynskipau","last":"Derczynski","locid":"785a7089-e58e-4aaa-986d-ae0e615ba508","name":"Derczynski, Leon","orcid":"0000-0002-8656-3431","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"3181-3197","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 59th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics and the 11th International Joint Conference on Natural Language Processing (Volume 1: Long Papers)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-274d2228-a805-4763-994d-7c2951a0c8a1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Annotating Online Misogyny","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381861","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Reading academic literature in digital formats is becoming more and more of a normalcy for students, but designers of reading support tools do not share common, metrics for evaluating such tools. This paper introduces our work in developing an evaluation form which we call the aRSX (active Reading Support IndeX). The aRSX-form is a quantitative means for evaluating whether a specific software or hardware tool supports active, academic reading in a way that resonates with personal user experience and learning preferences-in other words; whether the tool is practical and pleasant to use for the student who consumes academic literature. The paper presents the first and second iterations of the aRSX evaluation survey based on a preliminary exploratory experiment with 50 university students. The paper also describes how the evaluation form can be developed and used by designers of reading support tools.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Barkhuus, Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381811"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Academic reading","Digital reading","Reading support tools","User experience evaluation","aRSX evaluation form"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"developing evaluation metrics for active reading support","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86106752/CSEDU_aRSX.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381811","identifier":[{"id":"85136984735","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-2407420d-8136-4e85-95c9-23edf33d8c1a"],"keyword":["Academic reading","Digital reading","Reading support tools","User experience evaluation","aRSX evaluation form"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-376990C54B027E0F139793C4F7A88E6C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.scitepress.org/Papers/2021/104067/104067.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86106752/CSEDU_aRSX.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise","key":"barkhuuspau","last":"Barkhuus","locid":"831421c0-027f-4a25-b795-ee2fce0c8d2a","name":"Barkhuus, Louise","orcid":"0000-0003-1306-249X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5220/0010406701770188","eisbn":["9789897585029"],"pages":"177-188","publisher":"SCITEPRESS Digital Library","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 13th International Conference on Computer Supported Education (CSEDU 2021)","volume":"1"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85136984735"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2407420d-8136-4e85-95c9-23edf33d8c1a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Developing Evaluation Metrics for Active Reading Support","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381811","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This paper presents a framework of opportunities and barriers/risks between the two research fields Natural Language Processing (NLP) and Human-Computer Interaction (HCI). The framework is constructed by following an interdisciplinary research-model (IDR), combining field-specific knowledge with existing work in the two fields. The resulting framework is intended as a departure point for discussion and inspiration for research collaborations.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Derczynski, Leon"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-385019"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Framework","Human Computer Interaction","Interdisciplinary research","Natural Language Processing","Research collaboration"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"an idr framework of opportunities and barriers between hci and nlp","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://www.aclweb.org/anthology/2021.hcinlp-1.16/","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-385019","identifier":[{"id":"85118938822","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ffe0cf16-be5c-4cb0-8a2e-1b4caeffaa30"],"keyword":["Framework","Human-Computer Interaction","Interdisciplinary Research","Natural Language Processing","Research Collaboration"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-F80735140226464C6BB2C14714CC53A5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://www.aclweb.org/anthology/2021.hcinlp-1.16/","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85902110/An_IDR_Framework_of_Opportunities_and_Barriers_between_HCI_and_NLP.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon","key":"derczynskipau","last":"Derczynski","locid":"785a7089-e58e-4aaa-986d-ae0e615ba508","name":"Derczynski, Leon","orcid":"0000-0002-8656-3431","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"101-108","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","subtitle":"HCINLP","title":"Proceedings of the First Workshop on Bridging Human–Computer Interaction and Natural Language Processing"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ffe0cf16-be5c-4cb0-8a2e-1b4caeffaa30"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"An IDR Framework of Opportunities and Barriers between HCI and NLP","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-385019","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This paper presents and evaluates Aiki, a simple browser extension designed to redirect a user to a learning platform for a fixed amount of time before accessing websites defined as ’procrastination’ or ’time-wasting’ websites. The goal of the extension is to enable the user to exchange time spent on pages they believe contribute less to their own productivity for microlearning activities, defined as small or short-term learning activities. The paper describes the design and development of Aiki and evaluates the extension with a group of n = 10 participants studying the Danish language. Based on a two-week study, we conclude that this type of extension, even in its preliminary version, has the potential to improve language skills in a lightweight manner and that redirection is an important alternative to blocking for procrastination management.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Lungu, Mircea"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384034"],"event":{"acronym":"CHI","title":"ACM Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Aiki","Browser extension","Educational technology","Language learning","Microlearning","Procrastination management","Productivity enhancement","Redirection strategy","Time management","User study"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"aiki turning online procrastination into microlearning","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86144519/Aiki_turning_online_procrastination_into_microlearning.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-384034","identifier":[{"id":"85106686859","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-bd5b2ca4-00d7-470d-9d48-128c55c46593"],"keyword":["Aiki","Browser extension","Educational technology","Language learning","Microlearning","Procrastination management","Productivity enhancement","Redirection strategy","Time management","User study"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-10D3D295CF59E08C56293FE1D8EAA2A3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86144519/Aiki_turning_online_procrastination_into_microlearning.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mircea","key":"lungupau","last":"Lungu","locid":"02960d47-7677-4b2c-99d4-490a31fd6885","name":"Lungu, Mircea","orcid":"0000-0003-3944-5261","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3411764.3445202","pages":"1-13","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI '21: Proceedings of the 2021 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85106686859"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-bd5b2ca4-00d7-470d-9d48-128c55c46593"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Aiki - Turning Online Procrastination into Microlearning","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384034","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Academic reading, a form of active reading, often involves interaction with the text. Highlighting and annotating are some of the most common strategies of interacting with academic texts, yet we have limited understanding of exactly how such interactions affect reading comprehension in digital versus analog reading environments. In this paper, we present an exploratory study, comparing how university students (n = 50) interact with a digital and a physical text, focusing on highlights and annotations. We compare reading time, interaction with, and subsequent memory of the texts. We make nine observations about reading time, highlighting and annotation frequency and memory scores between paper and laptop. We find that students annotate significantly less on laptop than on paper, but that neither highlighting nor annotations influence subsequent memory of the text in either condition. Our broader contribution is to augment understanding of how different interaction features aid academic reading in a natural environment.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Barkhuus, Louise","Brabrand, Claus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384207"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Academic reading","Active reading support","Digital reading","Text annotation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"interacting with academic readings a comparison of paper and laptop","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86600745/1_s2.0_S2590291121001224_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384207","identifier":[{"id":"85136957631","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-c8877010-89b2-41dd-8f9f-b07f03b2724e"],"keyword":["Academic reading","Active reading support","Digital reading","Text annotation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-67BB47A184955140C2A61F8369D2C963"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86600745/1_s2.0_S2590291121001224_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise","key":"barkhuuspau","last":"Barkhuus","locid":"831421c0-027f-4a25-b795-ee2fce0c8d2a","name":"Barkhuus, Louise","orcid":"0000-0003-1306-249X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Claus","key":"brabrandpau","last":"Brabrand","locid":"47a94200-3abe-4354-b5e3-c8ef6f1ba1f2","name":"Brabrand, Claus","orcid":"0000-0001-5093-5806","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["25902911"],"journal":"Social Sciences & Humanities Open","paperid":"100226","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Social Sciences & Humanities Open"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85136957631"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-c8877010-89b2-41dd-8f9f-b07f03b2724e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Interacting with academic readings — A comparison of paper and laptop","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384207","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This paper presents a grounded theory-analysis based on a qualitative study of professional interaction designers (n=20) with a focus on how they use tools to manage design ideas. Idea management can be understood as a subcategory of the field Personal Information Management, which includes the activities around the capture, organization, retrieval, and use of information. Idea management pertains then to the management and use of ideas as part of creative activities. The paper identifies tool-supported idea management strategies and needs of professional interaction designers, and discusses the context and consequences of these strategies. Based on our analysis, we identify a conceptual framework of ten strategies which are supported by tools: saving, externalizing, advancing, exploring, archiving, clustering, extracting, browsing, verifying, and collaborating. Finally, we discuss how this framework can be used to characterize and analyze existing and novel idea management tools.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Dalsgaard, Peter"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-209110"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Design ideas","Design process","Design tools","Idea management","Ideation"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how interaction designers use tools to manage ideas","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3365104","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-209110","identifier":[{"id":"85083234116","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-8bd991b4-d275-4b6b-ba0e-0813ff38cbca"],"keyword":["Design ideas","Design process","Design tools","Idea management","Ideation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B8D2769F492E7334E673B371FE21CA10"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.08139.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3365104","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Humanities (HUM)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Arts, School of Communication and Culture, School of Communication and Culture - Department of Digital Design and Information Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"3d920ab1-3bf7-4959-b38f-7460123101c4","name":"Inie, Nanna","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"07bc29a5-f5da-47d0-8290-fd9ab3659541","name":"Dalsgaard, Peter","orcid":"0000-0001-9884-7178","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3365104","issn":["10730516","15577325"],"issn_org":["10730516"],"issue":"2","journal":"ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction","paperid":"7","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction","volume":"27"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85083234116"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-8bd991b4-d275-4b6b-ba0e-0813ff38cbca"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"How Interaction Designers Use Tools to Manage Ideas","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-209110","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Spread of misinformation presents a technological and so- cial threat to society. With the advance of AI-based lan- guage models, automatically generated texts have be- come difficult to identify and easy to create at scale. We present the “Rumour Mill\", a playful art piece, designed as a commentary on the spread of rumours and automatically- generated misinformation. The mill is a tabletop interactive machine, which invites a user to experience the process of creating believable text by interacting with different tangible controls on the mill. The user manipulates visible parame- ters to adjust the genre and type of an automatically gener- ated text rumour. The Rumour Mill is a physical demonstra- tion of the state of NLP technology and its ability to gener- ate and manipulate natural language text, and of the act of starting and spreading rumours.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Falk Olesen, Jeanette","Derczynski, Leon"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382325"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["AI based language models","Misinformation","Natural Language Processing NLP","automated text generation","interactive machine"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the rumour mill making the spread of misinformation explicit and tangible","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84873742/Rumour_Mill_CHI2020_extended_abstract.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382325","identifier":[{"id":"85090187632","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-472705ce-0e79-4f50-898f-847771c1d6ca"],"keyword":["AI-based language models","automated text generation","interactive machine","misinformation","natural language processing (NLP)"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-A0A2E3124158E55649E91066BC11B5F5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84873742/Rumour_Mill_CHI2020_extended_abstract.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Computing Education Research","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"e23e8096-59cb-4ef6-af48-ce9d806fd1eb","name":"Inie, Nanna","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-9542","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeanette","key":"falkolesenpau","last":"Falk Olesen","name":"Falk Olesen, Jeanette","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon","key":"derczynskipau","last":"Derczynski","locid":"785a7089-e58e-4aaa-986d-ae0e615ba508","name":"Derczynski, Leon","orcid":"0000-0002-8656-3431","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3334480.3383159","eisbn":["9781450368193/20/04"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"The ACM CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85090187632"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-472705ce-0e79-4f50-898f-847771c1d6ca"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Rumour Mill: Making the Spread of Misinformation Explicit and Tangible","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382325","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Misinformation spread presents a technological and social threat to society. With the advance of AI-based language models, automatically generated texts have become difficult to identify and easy to create at scale. We present \"The Rumour Mill\", a playful art piece, designed as a commentary on the spread of rumours and automatically-generated misinformation. The mill is a tabletop interactive machine, which invites a user to experience the process of creating believable text by interacting with different tangible controls on the mill. The user manipulates visible parameters to adjust the genre and type of an automatically generated text rumour. The Rumour Mill is a physical demonstration of the state of current technology and its ability to generate and manipulate natural language text, and of the act of starting and spreading rumours.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Falk Olesen, Jeanette","Derczynski, Leon"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-242499"],"event":{"acronym":"CHI","end":"2020-04-30","place":"Honolulu, us","start":"2020-04-25","title":"CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["cs CL","cs HC"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the rumour mill making the spread of misinformation explicit and tangible","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85090187632","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-242499","identifier":[{"id":"85090187632","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-c637936c-269a-4e9e-b6d6-209cab59ccc1"],"keyword":["cs.CL","cs.HC"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-A0A2E3124158E55649E91066BC11B5F5"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Arts, School of Communication and Culture, School of Communication and Culture - Department of Digital Design and Information Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","name":"Inie, Nanna","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeanette Falk","key":"olesenpau","last":"Olesen","locid":"8d0554b5-436a-4db6-85e2-eb0ad4cf341f","name":"Falk Olesen, Jeanette","name_org":"Olesen, Jeanette Falk","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon","key":"derczynskipau","last":"Derczynski","name":"Derczynski, Leon","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3334480.3383159","eisbn":["9781450368193"],"isbn":["9781450368193"],"place":"USA","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI EA 2020 - Extended Abstracts of the 2020 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85090187632"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-c637936c-269a-4e9e-b6d6-209cab59ccc1"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"The Rumour Mill : Making the Spread of Misinformation Explicit and Tangible","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-242499","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Elections are a time when communication is important in democracies, including over social media. This paper describes a case study of applying NLP to determine the extent to which misinformation and external manipulation were present on Twitter during a national election. We use three methods to detect the spread of misinformation: analysing unusual spatial and temporal behaviours; detecting known false claims and using these to estimate the total prevalence; and detecting amplifiers through language use. We find that while present, detectable spread of misinformation on Twitter was remarkably low during the election period in Denmark.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Derczynski, Leon","Albert-Lindqvist, Torben Oskar","Bendsen, Marius Venø","Inie, Nanna","Pedersen, Jens Egholm","Pedersen, Viktor Due"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381962"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Elections","Misinformation Detection","NLP","Social media","Twitter Analysis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"misinformation on twitter during the danish national election a case study","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84747296/paper_16.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381962","ids":["itu-2f158a6e-3156-4a0f-a28c-a55b1556f1d0"],"keyword":["Elections","Misinformation Detection","NLP","Social Media","Twitter Analysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-59237FBC317F886B5A4414757BB9EDB5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://truthandtrustonline.files.wordpress.com/2019/09/paper_16.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84747296/paper_16.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["Lix Technologies ApS"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTH","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon","key":"derczynskileonpau","last":"Derczynski","locid":"785a7089-e58e-4aaa-986d-ae0e615ba508","name":"Derczynski, Leon","orcid":"0000-0002-8656-3431","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Torben Oskar","key":"albertlindqvisttorbenoskarpau","last":"Albert-Lindqvist","name":"Albert-Lindqvist, Torben Oskar","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Marius Venø","key":"bendsenmariusvenøpau","last":"Bendsen","name":"Bendsen, Marius Venø","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"inienannapau","last":"Inie","name":"Inie, Nanna","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens Egholm","key":"pedersenjensegholmpau","last":"Pedersen","name":"Pedersen, Jens Egholm","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Viktor Due","key":"pedersenviktorduepau","last":"Pedersen","name":"Pedersen, Viktor Due","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the conference on Truth and Trust Online"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Other Companies","Private Research","Universities","lix technologies aps"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2f158a6e-3156-4a0f-a28c-a55b1556f1d0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Misinformation on Twitter During the Danish National Election: A Case Study","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381962","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Physical tools and materials like pen, paper, sticky-notes, and whiteboards are commonly used in collaborative creative design processes, whereas digital tools play a more marginal role. But what are the benefits and drawbacks of physical, digital, and hybrid physical-digital setups when it comes to supporting collaborative ideation? To answer this question, we present a study and analysis of three different implementations of a well-established collaborative ideation technique called Inspiration Card Workshop, with physical, digital, and hybrid setups. Each setup is a controlled experiment with three different groups of designers. We analyse the setups in terms of how they support five key aspects of collaborative design. Based on our insights, we present implications for future use of digital tools to support card-based collaborative design ideation, in which we argue for a technically lightweight hybrid workflow setup that builds on well-proven physical and digital components.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Lundqvist, Caroline Emilie","Klinkhammer, Daniel","Halskov, Kim","Feyer, Stefan Paul","Falk Olesen, Jeanette","Inie, Nanna","Reiterer, Harald","Dalsgaard, Peter"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-152846"],"event":{"acronym":"NordiCHI","title":"Nordic Conference on Human-Computer Interaction"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Konstanz"],"keyword":["Collaboration","Creativity support tools","Cross surface interaction","Design tools","Ideation"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"physical digital and hybrid setups supporting card based collaborative design ideation","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85056571224","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-152846","identifier":[{"id":"85056571224","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-28feee8b-9478-4240-ac66-07194a133249"],"keyword":["Collaboration","Creativity support tools","Cross-surface interaction","Design tools","Digital versus physical","Ideation"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-08454A88E197DDD83347DD8CE5674C5B"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Arts, School of Communication and Culture, School of Communication and Culture - CIBIS - Creativity in Blended Interaction Spaces"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"university of konstanz","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Konstanz","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Caroline Emilie","key":"lundqvistpau","last":"Lundqvist","locid":"11b15ba8-7197-461d-aac3-52b5b3203e7a","name":"Lundqvist, Caroline Emilie","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"klinkhammerpau","last":"Klinkhammer","name":"Klinkhammer, Daniel","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kim","key":"halskovpau","last":"Halskov","locid":"3043cd86-47ac-46ef-b907-a367b2f0ca5a","name":"Halskov, Kim","orcid":"0000-0001-7448-7577","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan Paul","key":"feyerpau","last":"Feyer","name":"Feyer, Stefan Paul","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeanette","key":"falkolesenpau","last":"Falk Olesen","locid":"8d0554b5-436a-4db6-85e2-eb0ad4cf341f","name":"Falk Olesen, Jeanette","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"3d920ab1-3bf7-4959-b38f-7460123101c4","name":"Inie, Nanna","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Harald","key":"reitererpau","last":"Reiterer","name":"Reiterer, Harald","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"07bc29a5-f5da-47d0-8290-fd9ab3659541","name":"Dalsgaard, Peter","orcid":"0000-0001-9884-7178","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3240167.3240177","eisbn":["9781450364379"],"isbn":["9781450364379"],"pages":"260-272","place":"USA","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","subtitle":"Revisiting the Life Cycle - Proceedings of the 10th Nordic Conference on Human-Computer Interaction","title":"NordiCHI 2018"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85056571224"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-28feee8b-9478-4240-ac66-07194a133249"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Physical, Digital, and Hybrid Setups Supporting Card-Based Collaborative Design Ideation","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-152846","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"We present the findings of a pilot study where we randomly resurfaced professional designers' own archived ideas to them over a period of three weeks. We find that resurfacing ideas can provide reflective and creative value to designers by encouraging them to reflect on their old ideas, reflect on themselves as practitioners, and as motivation to pick up forgotten or dormant ideas.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Dow, Steven","Dalsgaard, Peter"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-234782"],"event":{"acronym":"NordiCHI","title":"Nordic Conference on Human-Computer Interaction"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of California at San Diego"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"supporting reflective use of design idea archives using email","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85056590561","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-234782","identifier":[{"id":"85056590561","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-b8cf0f74-98c1-4b67-8e0f-dba4373b995b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DD9D5D9D9A762C30E7C39FB7322BD74B"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Arts, School of Communication and Culture, School of Communication and Culture - Centre for Advanced Visualisation and Interaction","Aarhus University, Arts, School of Communication and Culture, School of Communication and Culture - Department of Digital Design and Information Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of california at san diego","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California at San Diego","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"3d920ab1-3bf7-4959-b38f-7460123101c4","name":"Inie, Nanna","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Steven","key":"dowpau","last":"Dow","name":"Dow, Steven","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"07bc29a5-f5da-47d0-8290-fd9ab3659541","name":"Dalsgaard, Peter","orcid":"0000-0001-9884-7178","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3240167.3240251","isbn":["9781450364379"],"pages":"726-730","place":"USA","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"NordiCHI '18: 10th Nordic Conference on Human-Computer Interaction . New York : Association for Computing Machinery, 2018."},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85056590561"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-b8cf0f74-98c1-4b67-8e0f-dba4373b995b"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Supporting Reflective Use of Design Idea Archives Using Email","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-234782","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"This paper presents the results of an extensive qualitative study investigating how professional designers utilize personal idea archives. While we know that designers archive creative ideas in different formats and on different platforms, we know little about if and how designers utilize these idea archives in their daily practice. Through a series of interviews (n=20) and walkthroughs of design idea archives, we identified two archetypal strategies. The Problem Solver is concerned with the task at hand, keeps relevant ideas around, and discards them when the ideas have served their purpose. On the other hand, The Artisan Designer systematically archives potentially useful ideas in carefully selected formats, and continues developing ideas over extended time spans. We conclude with a discussion about how these different strategies might be supported by technological archiving tools.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Inie, Nanna","Endo, Allison","Dow, Steven","Dalsgaard, Peter"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-213310"],"event":{"acronym":"NordiCHI","title":"Nordic Conference on Human-Computer Interaction"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Tufts University","University of California at San Diego"],"keyword":["Archiving","Design ideas","Design practice","Idea management"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the problem solver and the artisan designer strategies for utilizing design idea archives","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85056582928","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-213310","identifier":[{"id":"85056582928","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-9369873e-9891-4a11-8864-56791ab466fc"],"keyword":["Archiving","Design ideas","Design practice","Idea management"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F81956060B2F70EC544E2C72C252B302"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Arts, School of Communication and Culture, School of Communication and Culture - Centre for Advanced Visualisation and Interaction","Aarhus University, Arts, School of Communication and Culture, School of Communication and Culture - Centre for Digital Creativity","Aarhus University, Arts, School of Communication and Culture, School of Communication and Culture - Department of Digital Design and Information Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"tufts university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Tufts University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of california at san diego","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California at San Diego","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanna","key":"iniepau","last":"Inie","locid":"3d920ab1-3bf7-4959-b38f-7460123101c4","name":"Inie, Nanna","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Allison","key":"endopau","last":"Endo","name":"Endo, Allison","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Steven","key":"dowpau","last":"Dow","name":"Dow, Steven","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"07bc29a5-f5da-47d0-8290-fd9ab3659541","name":"Dalsgaard, Peter","orcid":"0000-0001-9884-7178","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3240167.3240215","eisbn":["9781450364379"],"isbn":["9781450364379"],"pages":"397-406","place":"USA","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","subtitle":"Revisiting the Life Cycle - Proceedings of the 10th Nordic Conference on Human-Computer Interaction","title":"NordiCHI 2018"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85056582928"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-9369873e-9891-4a11-8864-56791ab466fc"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"The Problem Solver and The Artisan Designer: Strategies for Utilizing Design Idea Archives","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-213310","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
